@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — the person present who is not in sympathy
#
# One generalization, formed from two attested claims in two transmissions
# between which no route has been proposed: the Tungus performance recorded in
# nineteen thirty-five and the French animal magnetism of eighteen twenty-five.
#
# It is the near inverse of pmc:TheOneWhoStaysOutsideTheState and shares its
# figure: a second person who does not enter the state the operation consists
# in. There he is required and his absence prevents the operation; here his
# presence prevents it. The relation is stated at the end, because what decides
# between the two is not the figure but the office, and both statements turn on
# the same property of the outcome.
#
# The counter-search required by SPEC §16 was carried over the compiled holding
# before this was written, and it came back with a case that does not fall
# inside the scope. That case is the reason the statement has the clause it has.
#

pmc:TheUnsympatheticWitnessAsStatedCauseOfFailure a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The person present who is not in sympathy, named as the cause of failure"@en, "Der anwesende Unbeteiligte als genannte Ursache des Misslingens"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission has its operation performed by one person upon or through another, admits witnesses to it, and makes the outcome a condition of a person that only the operator or the subject can certify, it names the presence of a person not in sympathy with the operation as a sufficient cause of its failure, and prescribes the exclusion of such persons rather than a trial conducted before them. Where instead the outcome is a discrete event that any bystander present can read off independently of the operator's report, it names no such cause, admits the bystanders, and may build the check into the procedure."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:PerformanceFailsWithoutASusceptibleAudience ,
                       pmc:CautionAgainstWitnesses ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The two witnesses, each stating the condition in its own terms and each stating what follows. Among the Tungus, where too many of those present are not susceptible the audience reacts too slowly and the performance may fail; it may still be carried out formally, as a sequence of well known actions, without attaining its aim; and the presence of a single person who remains outside may suffice to disturb the rest. In the French manual only one witness is to be admitted, always the same and in communication with the operator, and all the curious and especially all the incredulous are to be banished, because where the stranger is incredulous, suspects the subject's sincerity or makes a jest of what he sees, the subject is troubled and loses his lucidity."@en ;
    skos:note "What the two have in common is not a complaint about scepticism, and the French case makes that plain: the witness the manual most sharply excludes is a physician who is not hostile but merely unfamiliar with the practice, on the ground that the subject would wish to convince him, would conduct his conversation with address, and would lose the simplicity his clairvoyance requires. The author gives a case of his own in which a sympathetic expert — a pupil of Mesmer, invited to instruct him — deranged the whole course of the somnambulism without touching the subject. The rule is about a person outside the communication and not about a person with an opinion."@en ;
    skos:note "The counter-search over the compiled holding, and the finding that shaped the statement. The Zande poison oracle is a transmission in which one man questions and another administers the poison to a fowl, the consultation is held in public, the bystanders may interrupt the questioner to have a point put that he has left out, and the transmission names eight causes to which a failure of the oracle is attributed — the wrong variety of the creeper, a breach of taboo, witchcraft, the anger of the owners of the forest, the age of the poison, the anger of the ghosts, sorcery, and use. Not one of the eight is a person present who is not in sympathy. The same transmission requires a second, corroborative consultation and holds two tests indispensable wherever the relations between two persons are at issue. That is a genuine opposite of the first clause and no counterexample to it, and the reason is the outcome: the fowl dies or it does not, and every bystander sees which."@en ;
    skos:note "So the deciding property is not the strangeness of the operation, nor the presence of witnesses, but whether the thing claimed can be established by somebody other than the parties to it. Where it can, the transmission has nothing to lose by an audience and gains a public record; where it cannot, the audience is the one circumstance under which the absence of the effect would have to be admitted, and the transmission accounts for that absence in advance. The second clause of the statement is the counter-search written into the claim rather than appended to it."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission of the first kind — one person operating on another, witnesses admitted, the outcome certifiable only by operator or subject — which names no unsympathetic witness among its causes of failure, or which prescribes that the operation be shown to a doubter as a proof. Sharper still, and the shape to look for first: one which states that the effect holds in the presence of a hostile observer. The corpora likeliest to carry it are the ones in this holding where a practitioner heals another before a village, and the Azande witch-doctor material and the Kongo and Ifa corpora have not been asked this question."@en ;
    skos:note "The weakness of the derivation, stated because a reader would otherwise have to find it. It stands on two claims, which is the floor the class sets and not a recommendation. The two are far apart and no route between them has been proposed, but they are not equally strong: the Tungus claim is a recorder's report of a stated cause of failure, with the recorder adding, as his own reading, that public opinion is nonetheless inclined to see the cause in the shaman; the French claim is a rule written by a practitioner for practitioners. A rule and a report are not the same kind of evidence for a statement about what transmissions provide, and the statement rests on the rule more heavily than on the report."@en ;
    skos:note "Its relation to pmc:TheOneWhoStaysOutsideTheState is inversion and not contradiction, and the two turn on one property between them. There the practitioner enters a condition in which he cannot check his own performance, so a second person outside it is required and the operation fails without him. Here the outcome cannot be checked from outside at all, so a second person outside the communication is excluded and the operation fails with him. Both statements are decided by where the capacity to check the operation lies, and a reader who wants to press either should press that: the first predicts a second person where the operator cannot monitor himself, the second predicts exclusion where nobody else can monitor the result."@en ;
    skos:note "What it does not say. It says nothing about whether any of these operations produces anything, and nothing about whether the presence or absence of such a person makes any difference to what they produce. It is a statement about the shape a transmission takes, it can be settled by reading, and that is why it stands here and not at a protocol."@en .
